## What is licensed how

Everything this project wrote is AGPL-3.0-only — every directory and every
language, with no dual licence. One set of material this project did not write
sits inside the tree and keeps its own terms: the vendored prose rules under
`.github/vale/styles/proselint/`. They are declared per file in `REUSE.toml`,
and `uvx --from "reuse==6.2.0" reuse lint` lists every licence the tree uses and
proves that no file is missing one.

Copyleft here is a choice rather than an inheritance: the scientific stack the
engine builds on is uniformly permissive, so no dependency compels any licence at
all. The Affero variant is chosen because the deployed form of this project is a
hosted compute engine, and section 13 obliges an operator who modifies it to
offer corresponding source to the users who reach it over a network. `LICENSE`
argues that in full before the licence text.

One constraint follows, and it is recorded here so that it is not rediscovered
during a dependency review: AGPL-3.0 is not compatible with GPL-2.0-only code,
so a GPL-2.0-only dependency can no longer be admitted to this tree.


## The vendored prose rules

`.github/vale/styles/proselint/` holds the proselint style, copied into this
repository verbatim so that the prose gate runs without a network fetch. It is
BSD-3-Clause, and it carries two sets of copyright: errata.ai, who publish the
Vale port, and Jordan Suchow, Michael Pacer and Lara A. Ross, who wrote the
original proselint. Both are declared in `REUSE.toml`.

BSD-3-Clause requires the copyright notice and the disclaimer to accompany every
redistribution, and publishing this repository is a redistribution. That is what
makes this paragraph an obligation rather than a courtesy. The upstream licence
text is retained beside the style, at
`.github/vale/styles/proselint/LICENSE`.

These are linting rules applied to the documentation. They are not linked into
the engine and form no part of the distributed program.


## Third-party packages in the image

The attribution register for the pinned packages the runtime image ships — every
package, its version and its declared licence — is `engine/sbom.cdx.json`, a
CycloneDX bill of materials. `README.md` and `LICENSE` name that same file and no
other. It is generated by `engine/scripts/gen_third_party.py` from the committed
`uv.lock` and the installed distribution metadata, and its `--check` mode
compares it byte for byte without writing. Count the components it declares
with:

    python3 -c "import json;print(len(json.load(open('engine/sbom.cdx.json'))['components']))"

Inside the image, each installed distribution keeps its own metadata and, where
it ships one, its own licence file, under the virtual environment's
`site-packages`.

CPython is distributed under the Python Software Foundation License, which is
permissive and GPL-compatible; its licence text ships with the interpreter.

Galaxy is MIT-licensed and will be an upstream dependency of `backend/`, never
vendored code; a permissive dependency combines with a copyleft work without
difficulty. `backend/` declares no dependency of any kind today, and
`.github/scripts/check-engine-boundary.sh` asserts that on every pull request.


## Corresponding source

Corresponding source for the copyleft components is available three ways.

1. **From the upstream index** — every package is pinned in `uv.lock` by exact
   name, version and hash. PyPI artifacts are immutable, so the pin identifies
   exact bytes, and sources are reachable per package at
   `https://pypi.org/project/<NAME>/<VERSION>/`.

2. **By reproducing the build** — `uv sync --locked` against the committed
   `uv.lock` retrieves the identical artifacts on any machine and verifies each
   one against the recorded hash.

3. **On request** — the Licensor will provide, for any recipient of the image
   and for at least three years, a complete machine-readable copy of the
   corresponding source of the copyleft components, on a medium customarily used
   for software interchange, for no more than the cost of distribution.

   Contact: open an issue titled "AGPL source request" at
   <https://github.com/Deadalus-Lab/EconFlow-Analytics/issues>. If the repository
   is ever unavailable, use GitHub's private vulnerability reporting on the same
   repository, which reaches the maintainers directly. A channel is published
   rather than an email address, which would be permanently harvested; the
   obligation is unchanged, and a request made through it is answered on the
   terms above.


## Network use

Section 13 is the operative clause for anyone running this software as a service.
If you modify EconFlow Analytics and let users interact with it remotely over a
network, you must offer those users the corresponding source of your modified
version. Deploying without distributing a binary does not avoid the obligation.
That is the entire point of choosing the Affero variant.

The engine's own source — every wrapper module, the gates and the tests — is in
this repository, licensed AGPL-3.0-only. See `LICENSE`.
